        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          <div>OBJECTIVES:</div><div>Recent evidence shows that radiation encountered during deep space travel is associated with increased risks of cancer, cardiovascular disease, and adverse effects in the central nervous system. A countermeasure delivered orally before and potentially during the missions beyond low earth orbit is a promising option to reduce the risk of carcinogenesis and degenerative effects in the heart and brain. Gamma-tocotrienol is one of the strongest radiation protectors of all natural compounds tested to date. It is non-toxic, well tolerated, and exhibits no interactions with other medications. With its anti-oxidant and anti-inflammatory properties, it protects against endothelial dysfunction and reduces radiation-induced genomic instability when studying in human endothelial cells and murine bone marrow cells. <br><br>Based on its safety profile, biological properties and preliminary results, gamma-tocotrienol has high potential as radiation countermeasure during space travel. The experiment will use a mouse model of exposure to simulate galactic cosmic rays to test whether gamma-tocotrienol protects against 1) carcinogenesis, 2) changes in behavior, dendritic morphology and synaptic plasticity, and 3) changes in cardiac vascular function and structure. Carcinogenesis will be assessed in genetically modified mice that show a low spontaneous cancer rate but increased tumor incidence in response to low-dose radiation. Male and female adult mice will be exposed to mixed charged particle beams or gamma-rays as a reference radiation, in five once-a-day fractions at the NASA Space Radiation Laboratory. Twenty-four hours before each radiation exposure, mice will receive an oral dose of gamma-tocotrienol. Mice will be followed for 18 months after irradiation and inspected daily for tumor formation. Bone marrow cells will be collected to assess the effects of gamma-tocotrienol on genomic instability by cytogenetic analysis. Additional cohorts of mice will be used to assess cognitive and cardiovascular function. At 12 months after irradiation, mice in these subcohorts will be sacrificed to collect blood samples, and brain and heart tissue for histological analysis. These studies will advance the countermeasure readiness level of gamma-tocotrienol against risks of both carcinogenesis and degenerative tissue effects of space radiation.<br><br>This study will focus on 3 specific aims:<br>1. Determine the efficacy of gamma-tocotrienol as a countermeasure against mixed charged particle radiation-induced carcinogenesis.<br>2. Determine the effects of gamma-tocotrienol on mixed charged particle radiation-induced degenerative effects on the central nervous system.<br>3. Determine the effects of gamma-tocotrienol on mixed charged-particle radiation-induced degenerative effects on the cardiovascular system.<br></div><div><br></div><div>APPROACH:</div><div>To measure the effects of gamma-tocotrienol as a countermeasure, a multifaceted approach will be used to evaluate the three specific aims of this study. Four-month old male and female Trp53?P mice will be exposed to a mixed field of charged particles (60% protons (250 MeV), 20% 4He (250 MeV/n), 10% 16O (350 MeV/n), 10% 28Si (260 MeV/n)) at the NSRL at BNL. The mixed field will be administered in 5 fractions of 0.15 Gy each over 5 consecutive days to mimic chronic low-dose radiation exposure. Separate cohorts of mice will be exposed to 5 fractions of ?-rays (0.2 Gy/fraction) as a reference at BNL. <br><br>Mice will be housed at BNL from 8 days before until 2 days after irradiation. At all other times, mice will be housed at CSU or UAMS. Both institutions have dedicated animal care facilities with full-time veterinarians on staff to assist with husbandry and animal health issues. Echocardiography and left ventricular catheterization will be performed under isoflurane inhalational anesthesia. Animals will be anesthetized with isoflurane inhalation, followed by exsanguination and removal of the heart and brain. Some mice in the carcinogenesis arm of the project will be euthanized by CO2 inhalation. Mice will be euthanized and necropsied when they become moribund, show evidence of tumor development, or reach 18 months post-irradiation. All tumors will be characterized by histopathology by a board certified veterinary pathologist. <br><br>Cardiac function will be measured by echocardiography. In addition, electrocardiograms (ECGs) will be recorded by dedicated ECG leads. At the predetermined endpoints of the studies, a subset of animals will undergo left ventricular catheterization to obtain pressure-volume loops measurements as a direct assessment of in vivo cardiac function while under inhaled isoflurane anesthesia, followed by collection of blood, heart, aorta, mesenteric and cerebral arteries, and brain. Separate groups of mice housed at UAMS will be injected with bromodeoxyuridine (BrdU) and biotinylated lectin before sacrifice, to permit image-analysis of neurogenesis and the microvasculature in collected tissues. BrdU and lectin can be injected in the same mice.<br></div><div><br></div><div>RESULTS:</div><div>This experiment is currently in progress, results will be available at a later date.<br></div>
